WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:03.719
BETH: An individual with autism
communicates differently to their

00:00:03.719 --> 00:00:07.939
peers often responding to verbal
communication literally and they

00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:12.309
also use their behaviour as a way of
communicating their feelings and emotions.

00:00:12.790 --> 00:00:16.040
It is helpful when communicating
with a person with autism spectrum

00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:20.000
disorder to be aware of their likely
tendency towards concrete thinking.

00:00:20.950 --> 00:00:24.010
They often don’t understand that
other people have different thoughts,

00:00:24.259 --> 00:00:28.110
desires and needs to them and cannot
put themselves in someone else’s shoes.

00:00:28.599 --> 00:00:31.650
They have difficulty predicting
and interpreting the behaviours of

00:00:31.679 --> 00:00:35.459
others and may also have trouble
understanding the effects of their own

00:00:35.590 --> 00:00:37.480
behaviour on the people around them.

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:41.589
JOE: Individuals with autism often
have difficulty recognising and

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:45.600
understanding social cues and
therefore do not instinctively

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:49.160
learn to adjust their behaviour
to suit different social contexts.

00:00:49.719 --> 00:00:53.559
Their behaviour however may sometimes
exhibit itself as challenging.

00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:58.510
This challenging behaviour is often a way
of showing their frustration anxiety or

00:00:58.510 --> 00:01:01.130
confusion or a way of getting attention.

00:01:01.610 --> 00:01:04.950
BETH: Behaviour exhibited by
individuals with autism can often

00:01:04.950 --> 00:01:08.110
be deemed as inappropriate by
their peers or others around them.

00:01:08.549 --> 00:01:11.899
This may involve them standing too
close, not showing eye contact,

00:01:12.129 --> 00:01:15.899
interrupting conversations and not
sticking to the usual social etiquette.

00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:20.470
If others are not aware of their
autism these behaviours can seem rude.

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:25.220
JOE: If an individual has sensory
problems, they may cause them to exhibit

00:01:25.259 --> 00:01:30.280
repetitive behaviours and actions such
as hand flapping, flicking, spinning,

00:01:30.699 --> 00:01:36.120
jumping and rocking or respond adversely
to sensory stimuli, this can bewilder

00:01:36.130 --> 00:01:38.520
others who are unaware or unsure of these

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:39.130
behaviours.

00:01:39.380 --> 00:01:42.249
BETH: It is therefore important
to know what the function of that

00:01:42.250 --> 00:01:46.701
behaviour is and try to deal with it
as all behaviours serve a purpose.

00:01:46.701 --> 00:01:49.720
If a trend is recognised, then
that will be the reason for the

00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:53.140
behaviour that regularly appears,
and it can help to see what the

00:01:53.140 --> 00:01:55.100
triggers are and how to deal with it.

00:01:55.900 --> 00:01:59.379
JOE: It is important then to be prepared
when communicating with an individual

00:01:59.389 --> 00:02:03.009
with autism that they have their
own individual challenges to social

00:02:03.009 --> 00:02:07.500
interaction and having some knowledge of
the potential for behavioural issues, can

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:11.660
help to prevent any situations becoming
more challenging through ignorance.

